namespace JuliaLean.Proofs.base.rational.Models1Group20

open JuliaLean JuliaLean.base.rational
open JuliaLean.base.rational.Models1Group20

/-!
Independent native rounded-division specifications.

The arithmetic specification uses unbounded integers only to describe exact
values. It never supplies an operation or correctness law to the model. The
rounding relation is given by bounding inequalities and distance/tie rules,
not by the source's divrem implementation. Input preconditions are precisely
`Normalized`, the existing rational representation invariant.

The contracts include every input of every represented native type. A failed
checked intermediate is part of the conclusion, never a precondition. Native
mixed unsigned/signed integer quotients may wrap after a sign conversion, so
successful results outside their mathematical representable range are checked
modulo the actual result width. The final operand facts also determine whether
division must fail: only a zero divisor or signed typemin divided by -1 raises
DivideError. Every other final division must succeed, including quotients which
wrap into an unsigned result type.
-/

/-- Mathematical membership in a native integer's representable range. -/
abbrev RoundedFits (t : IntTy) (z : Int) : Prop := t.min ≤ z ∧ z ≤ t.max

/-- The mathematical rounded integer, characterized independently of Julia's
integer division algorithm. The direction/tie rules are documented at
base/rounding.jl:48-110; `div` promises no intermediate rounding at
base/div.jl:5-13. A negative denominator is oriented positively only in this
mathematical specification. -/
def RoundedValue (a b : Int) (mode : Rounding) (q : Int) : Prop :=
  let n := if b < 0 then -a else a
  let d : Int := Int.ofNat b.natAbs
  let twiceDistance : Int := 2 * Int.ofNat (q * d - n).natAbs
  b ≠ 0 ∧
    match mode with
    | .toZero =>
        if n < 0 then (q - 1) * d < n ∧ n ≤ q * d
        else q * d ≤ n ∧ n < (q + 1) * d
    | .down => q * d ≤ n ∧ n < (q + 1) * d
    | .up => (q - 1) * d < n ∧ n ≤ q * d
    | .fromZero =>
        if n < 0 then q * d ≤ n ∧ n < (q + 1) * d
        else (q - 1) * d < n ∧ n ≤ q * d
    | .nearest =>
        twiceDistance ≤ d ∧ (twiceDistance = d → q % 2 = 0)
    | .nearestAway =>
        twiceDistance ≤ d ∧ (twiceDistance = d → n.natAbs ≤ (q * d).natAbs)
    | .nearestUp =>
        twiceDistance ≤ d ∧ (twiceDistance = d → n ≤ q * d)

/-- The exception class, with message text deliberately unspecified. -/
def RoundedInexact {t : IntTy} (run : Jl (JInt t)) : Prop :=
  ∃ message, run = .error (.inexactError message)

/-- The exception class promised by checked native arithmetic. -/
def RoundedOverflow {t : IntTy} (run : Jl (JInt t)) : Prop :=
  ∃ message, run = .error (.overflowError message)

/-- After the rational method's checked products, the remaining integer
operation rejects a zero divisor, returns every representable mathematical
rounded quotient, and preserves the rounded quotient modulo its native result
width on every successful return. Any final arithmetic failure is DivideError.
The specification covers unrepresentable quotients as well as fitting ones;
no hypothesis assumes that the model has succeeded. -/
def RoundedOutcome {t : IntTy} (run : Jl (JInt t))
    (a b : Int) (mode : Rounding) : Prop :=
  (b = 0 → run = .error .divideError) ∧
  (b ≠ 0 →
    (∀ q : Int, RoundedValue a b mode q → RoundedFits t q →
      ∃ out, run = .ok out ∧ out.val = q) ∧
    (∀ out, run = .ok out →
      ∃ q : Int, RoundedValue a b mode q ∧ out.val = wrapInt t q) ∧
    (∀ e, run = .error e → e = .divideError))

/-- The unique nonzero-divisor overflow of native signed integer division.
The two final operands are both signed and have been widened to the actual
signed result type. Its minimum divided by -1 is one above its maximum.
This is a mathematical boundary condition, not a call to any division model.
Julia source: base/int.jl:353-355,1085-1086;
src/runtime_intrinsics.c:1803 (pinned source citation in the existing intrinsic model). -/
def RoundedSignedQuotientOverflow (left right result : IntTy) (a b : Int) : Prop :=
  left.signed = true ∧ right.signed = true ∧ a = result.min ∧ b = -1

/-- Complete final native division outcome after the rational method's checked
products. The final operands are mathematically derived in the family contracts
below and must fit their actual input types. Zero divisor and the unique signed
overflow require DivideError for every rounding mode. Every other input requires
a successful result equal to the mathematical rounded quotient modulo the actual
result width; this includes unsigned results for negative mixed-sign quotients.

The condition uses no division-model call and does not repeat the source's
rounding algorithm. The expected quotient remains the independent inequality
and tie relation RoundedValue. Native signed/unsigned cases use unsigned
magnitude division and wrapping sign conversion, so their nonzero-divisor
quotients do not introduce a signed-overflow exception.
